# Venue: NeurIPS 2026, Main Track

Source of every requirement below: the world's venue library (`ht-role venue neurips`, retrieved by the library 2026-11-14), which cites the NeurIPS 2026 Call for Papers, Main Track Handbook, Reviewer Guidelines, the OpenReview submission form and the official style kit. I re-fetched the call and the handbook URLs on 2026-11-26 (HTTP 200) and the NeurIPS 2027 call (HTTP 404: no newer edition exists). I read the style kit's `neurips_2026.tex`, `.sty` and `checklist.tex` myself for the layout and checklist items. Planning estimates and sample statistics are marked *estimate* and are not venue rules.

## 1. The choice (the objective lets the study pick one of ICML, ICLR, NeurIPS)

**NeurIPS 2026 Main Track, contribution type "General".** Reasons, from the three venue records:

| | NeurIPS 2026 | ICLR 2027 | ICML 2027 |
|---|---|---|---|
| main text | 9 pages incl. figures and tables, one column (5.5 in) | 9 pages, one column (5.5 in) | 8 pages, two columns |
| mandatory checklist | yes (16 items, in the PDF) | no (AI-use statement only) | no (impact + AI-disclosure statements) |
| objective's wording ("the checklist the conference requires, e.g. NeurIPS") | met by the template's `checklist.tex` | nothing to fill in | nothing to fill in |
| newest edition with a published call | 2026 (2027 call is a 404) | 2027 | 2027 |

The study is an empirical evaluation-methodology paper with many figures and a heavy reproducibility story. NeurIPS gives it the most room for figures (one column) and a checklist that asks for exactly the things the objective's rubric asks for (error bars, compute, data licences, LLM use). The outline of `story-repo` already assumes 9 pages. ICML's 8 two-column pages would cost about a page of this paper.

The NeurIPS 2026 deadline (6 May 2026) has passed; this is a simulated submission, built to the 2026 rules because that is the newest NeurIPS edition with a call. If the paper is ever submitted for real, re-check the NeurIPS 2027 call.

## 2. Hard requirements (a breach is desk rejection unless marked otherwise)

**Length**
- Main text: **at most 9 content pages, figures and tables included.** Over the limit: "will not be reviewed" (`neurips_2026.tex`, §1.1).
- Not counted: references, acknowledgments, the paper checklist, optional technical appendices. No page limit on these. (A camera-ready adds one content page; not relevant to this delivery.)
- Reviewers are not required to read the appendix: the paper must stand alone in 9 pages. Anything a claim needs goes in the main text.

**Format (the style file enforces it; do not touch it)**
- US Letter. One column, text block 5.5 in × 9 in, left margin 1.5 in, top 1 in. Times (`ptm`), 10 pt on 11 pt leading, no paragraph indent.
- Do not change the style or its parameters, do not load `fullpage`, do not shrink margins, spacing or fonts: "tweaking the style files may be grounds for desk rejection".
- Smallest type sizes the style allows: `\tiny` ≥ 6 pt, `\scriptsize` ≥ 7 pt, `\footnotesize` ≥ 8 pt; references may use `\small` (9 pt). Captions and body are 10 pt.
- Fonts in the PDF: Type 1 or embedded TrueType only. Type 3 fonts are sent back for repair. **Matplotlib writes Type 3 by default: set `pdf.fonttype = 42`** (and `ps.fonttype = 42`) in every figure script.
- Pages are numbered (the style does it).
- Build mode for this delivery: `\usepackage{neurips_2026}` with **no** `final` or `preprint` option. That anonymises the author block, adds line numbers (do not refer to them) and the "Submitted to … Do not distribute" footer. Track option stays `main`.

**Anonymity (double blind)**
- No names, institutions, acknowledgments or funding in the paper (the `ack` environment hides itself in submission mode), none in any supplementary file or linked material, none in the code. A non-anonymous URL is a violation. Own prior work: third person.

**Mandatory parts**
- **Paper checklist**: `checklist.tex` of the kit, `\input` after references and appendix, inside the one PDF. Without it: desk reject. Keep the heading "NeurIPS Paper Checklist", answer each of the 16 questions with `\answerYes{}`, `\answerNo{}` or `\answerNA{}` and a 1–2 sentence justification (pointing to sections when Yes). Do not edit the questions; delete the instruction block. A "No" or "NA" with a justification is not grounds for rejection.
- **LLM-use disclosure**: checklist item 16, and a description in the experimental setup if LLM use is "an important, original, or non-standard component of the approach". The objective additionally requires LLM assistance to be disclosed in the paper. This study is carried out by LLM agents; say so plainly.
- Long-form lay summary, funding and competing interests: camera-ready only. Not needed.
- No section titled "broader impacts" is required; a societal discussion may be anywhere but counts against the 9 pages.

**Supplementary**
- Text appendix: inside the same PDF after the references. No separate appendix PDF.
- Code and data: one separate ZIP, **at most 100 MB**, anonymised. Should include evaluation code and a dependency specification, ideally self-contained and runnable (otherwise say why). Raw data does not go in (the objective forbids it too): the README gives source, version and sha256.
- The one-PDF file limit is 50 MB.
- Supplementary and appendix are optional reading for reviewers; supplementary stays visible after review.

**Submission mechanics (not part of this delivery, no role submits)**: OpenReview; the form asks for a confidential `LLM_usage` selection and confirmation that the checklist is in the PDF.

## 3. What reviewers judge

Review form: Summary; Strengths and Weaknesses over **Quality, Clarity, Significance, Originality** (each 1–4); 3–5 Questions; Limitations; Overall 1–6 (6 Strong Accept … 1 Strong Reject); Confidence 1–5. The checklist is one of the factors. Formatting violations are listed but must not lower the score.

Definitions (Reviewer Guidelines, paraphrased with the source's own key phrases):
- **Quality**: technically sound, claims well supported, methods appropriate, a complete piece of work, honest about strengths and weaknesses.
- **Clarity**: well written and organised; "enough information for an expert reader to reproduce its results".
- **Significance**: impactful for the community; others likely to build on it; unique data, conclusions or approaches count.
- **Originality**: new insights, deeper understanding, or "important properties of existing methods"; an evaluation of existing methods can count as much as a new method; clear distinction from prior work with citations.

**Contribution type is chosen at submission and cannot be changed.** Types: General ("most submissions"), Theory, Use-Inspired, Concept & Feasibility (high bar), **Negative Results** ("understanding a negative result"; significance and originality bars are high; must be "unexpected or surprising in some way", against popular belief). For this study:
- Declare **General**. The objective accepts "sensitivity is small", "it depends" and "unexpected" as results; under NeurIPS a null finding labelled *Negative Results* faces the higher bar and the "surprising" test. Under General, soundness, completeness and honest scope decide, and Originality may rest on a new measurement scale and a matched-contrast design applied to existing data and methods.
- What a General-type reviewer will therefore look for: that every claim in the abstract and introduction is supported by a reported measurement with uncertainty, that the scope is stated (checklist 1, 2, 7), that the contribution over prior work is explicit, and that the paper is complete rather than a pilot.

