## 5. Figures, tables, widths and type sizes (for `figtab-repo`)

- `\linewidth` = **5.5 in (396 pt)**: a full-width figure is 5.5 in; two panels side by side are about 2.7 in each (`0.49\linewidth`); three about 1.8 in each. Use `\includegraphics[width=…\linewidth]{…}` and vector PDF. No landscape pages in the main text.
- The venue states **no minimum for lettering inside figures**. The smallest size the style itself allows is 7 pt (`\scriptsize`), 6 pt (`\tiny`, floor). *Estimate/recommendation*: make text inside a figure at least 7 pt **at printed size** (i.e. draw at the width it prints at, 5.5 in or 2.7 in, not larger and scaled down), tick labels 7 pt or more, axis labels and legends 8 pt or more. Tables: body at `\small` (9 pt) or `\footnotesize` (8 pt; floor), never smaller.
- Captions are 10 pt Times set by the style. Figure: number and caption **below** the figure; table: caption **above** the table. Caption is lower case except for the first word and proper nouns; the kit asks every caption to "explain what it shows and add a key take-away message".
- Tables: centred, booktabs rules, **no vertical rules**. Error bars in tables or plots: state in the text what they are and how computed (checklist 7); for asymmetric quantities do not draw symmetric bars that leave the range (e.g. a flip probability below 0).
- Colour is allowed; the figure and caption should still read when printed in black and white (line style or marker as a second channel).
- Matplotlib: `pdf.fonttype = 42` (Type 3 is not accepted). Use a serif or Helvetica family consistent with the Times text.
- Every float counts in the 9 pages. See §7 for the budget.

## 6. How accepted papers write abstract and introduction, and what they hold

Basis: 13 NeurIPS 2023–2024 main-track papers on evaluation methodology, pairwise ranking/Elo, LLM-judge/preference evaluation and influence or data-removal analysis, picked by me by title from the proceedings index (not a random sample, not exhaustive); numbers measured from the proceedings PDFs by `accepted_sample/scripts/*.py`, table in `accepted_sample/sample.csv`. Details and caveats in `ACCEPTED_PAPERS.md`. They are camera-ready papers at the 10-content-page limit of those years, so counts for a 9-page submission should be read about 10 % lower.

**Abstract** (median ≈ 210 words, range 76 for a theory paper to 275). Order in the closest papers: (1) the object and why it is used ("Elo is increasingly used to evaluate LLMs through A-vs-B comparisons"; "LLMs are often ranked according to alignment with human preferences"); (2) the gap or surprise ("However … suitability remains unexplored"; "Surprisingly, practitioners cannot measure the uncertainty"; "Surprisingly, … less than 60 %"); (3) what the paper does, with named quantities; (4) the evidence in words (data named, number of models or datasets); (5) the practical consequence or guideline. Numeric results appear in the abstract of only 2 of 13 (one gives a range of drops, one a threshold); the rest state findings in words and name the data.

**Introduction** (median ≈ 740 words ≈ 1.3 pages, range 580–1290). Pattern: a motivating paragraph with dense citations (several per sentence in the closest papers) on why the object matters; a gap paragraph ("to date no comprehensive examination …"); "This study / In this paper we …" with the approach; **the findings stated in the introduction in words** (the Elo paper states its three findings there); an explicit contributions list or paragraph in most (11 of 13 introductions use the word "contribution"; bulleted lists were detected in 2, others write it as a paragraph); implications or concurrent-work notes for the closest ones. Figure 1 is on page 2 in 7 of 13 papers; it is the teaser or overview figure.

**Main text contents**
- Captioned figures in the main text: median 4 (range 1–8, mean 4.1). Captioned tables: median 0 (range 0–4, mean 0.8). Figures plus tables: median 4, range 1–8. Multi-panel figures count once.
- Distinct works cited in the main text: median 47 (range 24–116, middle half 39–75); bibliography 37–120 entries (median 50). Related work is its own top-level section in 8 of 13 papers (in the others it sits in the introduction, a subsection or the appendix).
- Main text length: 4,800–6,700 words extracted from the PDF (this includes figure text and equations), median ≈ 6,200.
- A heading with "Limitations" appears in 4 of 13 (others discuss them in the conclusion or not at all in a named place); NeurIPS 2026 asks for a limitations discussion in the checklist (item 2) and tells reviewers not to penalise honesty about them.

